#speculation(title: [Cycle-Timed Mast-Cell Dosing])[
Estrogen can augment mast-cell activity: estradiol activates mast cells through a non-genomic estrogen receptor alpha and calcium influx in vitro @Zaitsu2007EstradiolMastCell, and GPER-mediated estrogen signalling is linked to mast-cell tryptase and histamine expression in a rat model of visceral hypersensitivity @Xu2020GPERMastCell. In mice, mast cells accumulate in somatosensory thalamic nuclei during estrus @Kovacs2006EstrusMastCell. The human evidence is epidemiological and review-level: sex hormones are associated with immediate-type hypersensitivity @Chen2008SexHormones, with urticaria @KasperskaZajac2008SexHormonesUrticaria, and with respiratory and skin allergies more broadly @Ridolo2019SexAllergies. The full estrogen–progesterone–mast-cell axis is treated elsewhere in this volume (@sec:fra-hrt-mastcell); this speculation concerns only the dosing implication. If estrogen augments mast-cell degranulation, then MCAS-type symptoms in premenopausal women should track the cycle, worsening around the estrogen peak, and a mast-cell-drug schedule synchronised to cycle phase could outperform fixed daily dosing. The translation gap is large: the causal estrogen–mast-cell data are in vitro and animal, the human data are associative, no study has shown that MCAS or ME/CFS symptoms track menstrual phase in a verified way, and no trial has compared cycle-synchronised against fixed mast-cell dosing. (Certainty: 0.30)

_Falsifiable prediction:_ In premenopausal women with MCAS-type ME/CFS symptoms, prospectively recorded symptom severity (flushing, pruritus, gastrointestinal, pain) is higher in the late-follicular-to-ovulatory phase than in the luteal phase, and a cycle-synchronised adjustment of mast-cell therapy reduces that phasic worsening more than a fixed daily schedule.

*Consequence:* If confirmed, menstrual-cycle phase would become a variable to control in research on mast-cell therapy in premenopausal patients. Until a cycle-timing trial exists, this is a testable hypothesis, not a dosing recommendation.
] <spec:cycle-timed-mast-cell-dosing>
